WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.180
Work in progress is the set of active commitments that consume attention, tracking, coordination, or emotional load.

2
00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:17.160
It includes tasks currently being executed, partially completed work, waiting items that still need monitoring, and open loops that remain unresolved.

3
00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:20.220
WIP is not free just because a task is paused.

4
00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:25.680
Paused work still occupies space in memory, systems, and conversations.

5
00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:28.440
Open loops are especially expensive.

6
00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:37.500
An open loop might be an unfinished email, a decision waiting for input, a half-written document, an unreviewed pull request, or a commitment with no next action.

7
00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:43.600
Each loop creates a small demand to remember, check, follow up, or worry.

8
00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:48.100
Too many loops make the system feel heavier than the visible workload suggests.

9
00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:56.700
WIP limits come from kanban thinking, but the idea is simple enough for any work system: limit the number of active commitments so more work can actually finish.

10
00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:00.560
Starting too much creates switching overhead and hides blockage.

11
00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:09.460
Finishing before starting reduces coordination burden, makes progress visible, and creates room for the next priority without carrying as many half-finished obligations.

12
00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:15.340
A WIP limit is not a rule for never helping anyone or never adapting.

13
00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:17.680
It is a boundary that makes overload visible.

14
00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:22.340
When the limit is reached, the next conversation is not simply how do we add more.

15
00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:28.440
It is what should finish, pause, move, delegate, split, or stop.

16
00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:33.980
Controlling active commitments is one of the most practical ways to reduce multitasking pressure.
